{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Melanoma tumors with Breslow thickness above the cohort median exhibit higher expression of epithelial–mesenchymal transition (EMT) genes (VIM, SNAI1, TWIST1, ZEB1) compared to tumors with Breslow thickness at or below the median.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether high‐Breslow tumors show upregulation of canonical EMT markers relative to low‐Breslow tumors.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Compute the median of breslow_thickness across patients with available values.  3. Assign each patient to High‐Breslow (> median) or Low‐Breslow (≤ median).  4. Map EMT gene symbols (VIM, SNAI1, TWIST1, ZEB1) to Ensembl IDs using mygene or a gene‐mapping table; subset the expression matrix to these genes.  5. Aggregate sample‐level expression to patient‐level if multiple samples exist (e.g., by mean).  6. For each EMT gene, perform a Wilcoxon rank‐sum test comparing High vs Low groups; compute fold‐changes and adjust p‐values by Benjamini–Hochberg.  7. Compute an aggregate EMT score per patient (e.g., average z‐score across genes), compare the score between groups via Wilcoxon and visualize with boxplot.  8. Summarize results.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of per‐gene mean expression by group, test statistic, raw p‐value, FDR.  • Boxplots of expression for each EMT gene by Breslow group.  • Boxplot and statistical summary for the aggregate EMT score.  • Interpretation of whether high‐Breslow tumors are enriched for EMT phenotype."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients with NF1‐loss‐of‐function mutations exhibit higher expression of cytotoxic immune markers (CD8A, GZMB, PRF1) compared to NF1 wild‐type patients, suggesting increased immune infiltration.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between NF1 mutational status and expression of T cell cytotoxicity genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Identify patients harboring any NF1 mutation classified as Missense_Mutation, Nonsense_Mutation, or Splice_Site; label them NF1‐mutant vs NF1‐WT.  3. Map CD8A, GZMB, PRF1 to Ensembl IDs and subset expression data; aggregate to patient‐level if needed.  4. Merge patient‐level expression with NF1 status.  5. For each immune gene, perform two‐sample t‐tests or Wilcoxon tests comparing mutant vs WT; adjust for multiple testing.  6. Optionally, compute an “immune cytotoxicity signature” score (mean z‐score across genes) and test between groups.  7. Visualize distributions with violin or boxplots.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of per‐gene expression means, test statistics, p‐values, FDR.  • Signature score comparison and statistical summary.  • Plots (violin/box) of expression by NF1 status.  • Brief report on whether NF1‐mutant tumors show elevated cytotoxic marker expression."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Tumors with histologic ulceration (ulceration_indicator=YES) have higher tumor mutational burden (TMB) than non‐ulcerated tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if ulcerated melanomas carry greater somatic mutation load.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_clinical_data.csv.  2. For each patient, count nonsynonymous mutations (Variant_Classification in {Missense_Mutation, Nonsense_Mutation, Splice_Site}) and divide by exome size (30 Mb) to compute TMB.  3. From clinical data, extract ulceration_indicator and restrict to patients with non‐missing YES/NO.  4. Merge TMB with ulceration status.  5. Compare TMB distributions between ulcerated vs non‐ulcerated via Wilcoxon rank‐sum test and compute effect size.  6. Plot TMB boxplots or violin plots by ulceration status.",  
      "deliverables":"• Table of sample counts, median TMB by ulceration status.  • Wilcoxon test statistic, p‐value, and effect size (e.g., Cliff’s delta).  • Boxplot/violin plot of TMB by group.  • Conclusion on association between ulceration and TMB."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients who received radiation therapy (radiation_therapy=YES) have a higher frequency of mutations in DNA damage response genes (ATM, TP53, BRCA1) compared to patients without radiation.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether prior radiation correlates with enrichment of mutations in key DNA repair genes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. From clinical data, define Radiation vs No‐Radiation cohorts (radiation_therapy=YES vs NO).  3. In the mutation dataset, flag patients with at least one mutation in ATM, TP53, or BRCA1.  4. Construct a contingency table for each gene: mutated vs not mutated × Radiation vs No‐Radiation.  5. Perform Fisher’s exact test for each gene to assess enrichment.  6. Adjust p‐values for multiple comparisons; compute odds ratios.",  
      "deliverables":"• Contingency tables for ATM, TP53, BRCA1.  • Fisher’s exact test p‐values, odds ratios, and FDR.  • Barplots of mutation frequencies by therapy group.  • Interpretation of whether radiation‐treated tumors harbor more DNA repair gene mutations."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Somatic mutations in MUC16 are associated with altered MUC16 mRNA expression levels in melanoma tumors.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Investigate whether MUC16 mutation status correlates with its own expression.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_mutations.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Identify patients with any MUC16 mutation (Hugo_Symbol=MUC16) and label them MUC16‐mutant vs MUC16‐WT.  3. Map MUC16 gene symbol to Ensembl ID and extract its expression per sample; aggregate to patient‐level if needed.  4. Merge expression data with mutation status.  5. Compare MUC16 expression between mutant and WT groups using two‐sample t‐test or Wilcoxon test.  6. Plot boxplot of MUC16 expression by mutation status.",  
      "deliverables":"• Number of patients in each group.  • Mean expression, test statistic, p‐value, and FDR for MUC16.  • Boxplot of MUC16 expression by status.  • Summary statement on whether MUC16 mutations impact transcript level."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Gene expression profiles of melanoma samples from primary skin sites cluster distinctly from those of lymph node metastases, reflecting site‐specific transcriptional programs.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Use unsupervised clustering to test if primary_site (Skin vs Lymph nodes) corresponds to distinct expression clusters.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_gene_expression.csv.  2. Subset clinical to patients with primary_site Skin or Lymph nodes.  3. Extract their expression matrices; filter to the top 5,000 most variable genes by variance.  4. Perform principal component analysis (PCA) or t-SNE on the filtered matrix.  5. Color samples by primary_site on the first two PCs (or t-SNE axes); visually assess separation.  6. Perform k-means clustering (k=2) on the same data.  7. Compute a contingency table between cluster assignments and primary_site; test association via chi‐squared test.",  
      "deliverables":"• PCA/t-SNE scatterplot colored by site.  • Cluster vs site contingency table, chi‐squared p‐value.  • Cluster centroids’ top contributing genes.  • Conclusion on whether site stratifies global expression patterns."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Older melanoma patients (age_at_diagnosis > 65 years) have higher tumor mutational burden compared to younger patients (≤ 65 years).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess the relationship between age at diagnosis and TMB.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv and cleaned_mutations.csv.  2. Calculate each patient’s TMB as nonsynonymous mutation count per 30 Mb.  3. From clinical data, assign patients to Older (> 65) vs Younger (≤ 65) groups.  4. Merge TMB with age group labels.  5. Compare TMB distributions between groups using Wilcoxon test; compute median and IQR.  6. Additionally, compute Spearman correlation between age_at_diagnosis and TMB as a continuous analysis.  7. Plot scatter of age vs TMB with regression line and boxplots by group.",  
      "deliverables":"• Wilcoxon test statistic, p‐value, median TMB by age group.  • Spearman rho and p‐value.  • Boxplots and scatter plot with trend line.  • Interpretation of age–TMB relationship."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Patients who develop subsequent primary melanomas during follow‐up have shorter overall survival compared to those who do not develop subsequent primaries.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine if occurrence of new primary melanomas predicts poorer survival.",  
      "steps":"1. Load cleaned_clinical_data.csv.  2. Define two cohorts: Subsequent_Primary=YES (subsequent_primary_melanoma_during_followup=YES) vs NO.  3. Extract survival data (days_to_death, vital_status) and censoring information.  4. Fit Kaplan–Meier survival curves for both cohorts; plot curves.  5. Perform a log‐rank test to compare survival distributions.  6. Optionally, fit a Cox model adjusting for age and stage to estimate hazard ratio for subsequent primary event.",  
      "deliverables":"• KM survival curves for both groups.  • Log‐rank test statistic and p‐value.  • Cox model hazard ratio for subsequent primary indicator (if fitted).  • Table summarizing median survival times.  • Conclusion on prognostic impact of new primary melanoma events."  
    }  
  }  
]}
